      "code_sha256_lf": "10bf3c641700efcf2afdf6549e7f9c6b9e6bd9da99f26d173c0723068a57bc28",
      "rationale": "The reported regression (issue193/194 function splitting, CASE/WHEN procedure splitting, etc.) traces to StatementSplitter._handle_nested_block in sqlparse/engine/statement_splitter.py, where the WHILE check was inverted to `if not (unified == 'WHILE')`. This bug causes the method to intercept and return 0 for almost every keyword seen inside a BEGIN block - including the closing `END`, `END IF` and `END CASE` tokens - so `_handle_closing_keyword` (which should pop the block stack and return -1 to close the BEGIN block) is never reached. As a result the split level never drops back to 0, and sqlparse.split() incorrectly merges a CREATE FUNCTION/PROCEDURE's BEGIN...END body with the following statement into one. The tests build minimal BEGIN...END function/procedure bodies (plain, with an IF block, and with a CASE/WHEN block, mirroring the three named regressions) and assert that sqlparse.split() returns two statements split exactly at the function/procedure's closing END; - behavior that is evidently intended by the splitter's own docstrings/comments about BEGIN...END blocks and is restored once the WHILE condition is corrected to `if unified == 'WHILE'`.",
